>Do you mean Artifical Intelligence? or do you mean Artifically Human?
>The two are very different. In a game, AI is not all that hard to do. But 
>an artifically intelligent opponnent will go from point A to point B in 
>the shortest path every time which the human player will ambush. 

In my opinion, that isn't true Artifical Intellegence.  It's more like
scripted behaviour that doesn't vary (and as a result, feels like
everything else.)

If, however, the shortest path is not taken if an alternate tactic is more
beneficial, then that would be AI. As an example, I'll point to Angband
where the packs of enemies refuse to chase you down a corridor unless they
are certain that you can be killed. (although, as one poster put it, "Isn't
that a bit too calculated?")  It's still predictable, but is harder to
counter.

>Making 
>it smarter means adding randoms to its actions, even to the point of 
>doing an occassional surprising stupid move. 

While adding randomness is a way to make an AI smarter, there are ways to
improve the intellegence without excessive randomness.  Thus, an AI player
will change its tactics, but wont be prone to stupid mistakes - but there
will still be an element of randomness, whether it is from past performance
from a player plays or through minor weight tweaks done by the RNG.

I don't mind if AI players cheat as long as it doesn't need to rely on
those cheats to win.  However, there needs to be information on how much
these players cheat, such as specifing they they receive additional
resources, or have knowledge of eveything on the map. 

>Its hard to get an AI to play humanly. Especially if you try to limit it 
>to  having and "knowing" only what a human player in the game would be 
>able to have and know. 

It is my belief that it is possible for an AI to be strong without having
too much randomness or cheating.  As you know, an AI is only as strong as
the person writing it.

Personally, I think the AI in Rise of Nations is as strong as something
currently available.  While it doesn't appear to be random, it certainly
doesn't fall prey to ambushing on the shortest route either - it does seem
to probe your base for attack routes from the side, bypassing the array of
towers set up on the primary front.  (That, and when it chooses to do a
frontal assault, it simply sits back and launches artillery rather than
charging to certain death.)  

While I'm not certain the AI absolutly doesn't cheat, it does seem to
behave similar to a human player - thus any cheating done is either very
subtle, or not a true form of cheating (e.g. a real application of fuzzy
logic.)

>The cheap easy way to get a "human opponent" game 
>AI is to add randoms and to have it cheat. So far.

I would disagee.  As you know, an AI player for the game of Chess can be
extremely powerful, as it can do a brute-force examination of the board to
see 12 moves ahead.  If an AI for Chess can be so powerful, why can the AI
for other games be as well?

While the argument could be the fact that moden strategy games are too
complex for an AI to brute force all possibilities, it doesn't mean it's
impossible.  There are still ways to make AI processing much easier, such
as breaking the map into zones (which in turn contain information on choke
points, resources, etc.)  


